He described music as “therapy,” and nothing was better than playing with his bandmates Connor Brooker and Brock Weston.

&#8220;Especially when I&#8217;m really down, I&#8217;ll just play guitar as much as my fingers will allow,” he told Hack in 2025. &#8220;You&#8217;ll have to break my arms and peel my entire skin off before I&#8217;ll stop playing.&#8221;

ARIA and triple j paid tribute to the late artist. &#8220;Vale Jordan Brunoli, taken far, far too young,&#8221; reads a statement from the trade body. &#8220;He used his interviews with triple j’s Hack to tell young Australians to advocate for their health and heard back from some who’d been diagnosed early enough to be treated.&#8221;

Brunoli is survived by his wife George, whom he married earlier this year. In his most recent interview with Hack, which aired in August, Brunoli thanked those nearest and dearest.

&#8220;If I didn&#8217;t have Brock, Connor and my partner, G, keeping me grounded every day … I wouldn&#8217;t be anywhere near as comfortable as I am right now,&#8221; he remarked. &#8220;G and I were meant to be and always will be.&#8221;
